<article class="env-lemma" id="00G4">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00G4">Lemma <span data-tag="00G4">10.35.6</span>.</a> The ring $\mathbf{Z}$ is a Jacobson ring. More generally, let $R$ be a ring such that </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$R$ is a domain, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$R$ is Noetherian, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
any nonzero prime ideal is a maximal ideal, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$R$ has infinitely many maximal ideals. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then $R$ is a Jacobson ring. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $R$ satisfy (1), (2), (3) and (4). The statement means that $(0) = \bigcap _{\mathfrak m \subset R} \mathfrak m$. Since $R$ has infinitely many maximal ideals it suffices to show that any nonzero $x \in R$ is contained in at most finitely many maximal ideals, in other words that $V(x)$ is finite. By Lemma <a href="/tag/00E5" data-tag="00E5">10.17.7</a> we see that $V(x)$ is homeomorphic to $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R/xR)$. By assumption (3) every prime of $R/xR$ is minimal and hence corresponds to an irreducible component of $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R/xR)$ (Lemma <a href="/tag/00ES" data-tag="00ES">10.26.1</a>). As $R/xR$ is Noetherian, the topological space $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R/xR)$ is Noetherian (Lemma <a href="/tag/00FQ" data-tag="00FQ">10.31.5</a>) and has finitely many irreducible components (Topology, Lemma <a href="/tag/0052" data-tag="0052">5.9.2</a>). Thus $V(x)$ is finite as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>